entire session dictionary is serialized and written to the cookie (lines 387-398). If the session was modified to be empty, the cookie is deleted (lines 368-379).\\n\\n6. **Concurrent Request Handling**: As documented in `SessionInterface` (lines 141-146 in `src/flask/sessions.py`), multiple requests with the same session may be handled concurrently. Each request has its own isolated session instance loaded from the cookie at the start, modified independently in memory, and saved back to the cookie at the end. The callback mechanism ensures that modifications are accurately tracked per request, and only modified sessions trigger cookie updates, preventing unnecessary writes and maintaining consistency.\\n\\nThis design ensures that: (a) modifications are automatically detected without manual flag management, (b) each request\'s session state is isolated, (c) only modified sessions trigger cookie persistence, and (d) the in-memory state accurately reflects what gets persisted, maintaining consistency across concurrent requests."}' + '\n')
